The current benefits system in most countries is costly to administer, means-tested in ways that create poverty traps, and leaves many who need help outside its reach; UBI is simpler and reaches everyone.
Cash transfers outperform in-kind transfers (food stamps, housing vouchers) in empirical comparisons because recipients can meet their own most urgent needs rather than receiving what administrators think they need.
Alaska has run a form of UBI — the Permanent Fund Dividend — since 1982, consistently paying residents $1,000–$2,000/year from oil revenues; Alaska has the lowest poverty rates and highest social mobility of any US state.
The automation of routine work means labour income alone may be insufficient to maintain consumption for a growing share of the workforce; UBI provides a floor as the economy transitions.
Mental health benefits from financial security are economically significant: the American Institute for Stress estimates financial anxiety costs employers $500 billion annually in lost productivity.
